¶
'''abridgment''' (a̤-brij′ment), ''n.'' [< late ME. ''abrygement'', < OF. ''abrigement'', ''abregement'': see ''abridge'' and ''-ment''.] '''1.''' The act of abridging, or the state of being abridged; diminution; contraction; reduction; curtailment; restriction: as, an ''abridgment'' of expenses; “''abridgment'' of liberty,” ''Locke.''
{{smaller block|Persons employed in the mechanic arts are those whom the ''abridgment'' of commerce would immediately affect.
{{right|''A. Hamilton'', Works, II. 15.}}}}
{{smaller block|It was his sin and folly which brought him under that ''abridgment''.
{{right|''South.''}}}}
'''2.''' A condensation, as of a book; a reduction within a smaller space; a reproduction of anything in reduced or condensed form.
{{smaller block|A genuine ''abridgment'' is a reproduction of the matter or substance of a larger work in a condensed form, and in language which is not a mere transcript of that of the original.
{{right|''Drone'', Copyright, p. 158.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Here lies David Garrick, describe him who can,
An ''abridgment'' of all that was pleasant in man.</poem>
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Retaliation.}}}}
'''3.''' That which abridges or cuts short. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Look, where my ''abridgments'' come [namely, the players who cut me short in my speech. Compare, however, meaning 4].
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, ii. 2.}}}}
'''4.''' That which shortens anything, as time, or makes it appear short; hence, a pastime. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Say, what ''abridgment'' have you for this evening?
What mask, what music?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., v. 1.}}}}

Also spelled ''abridgement''.
{{smaller block|='''Syn. 2.''' ''Abridgment'', ''Compendium'', ''Epitome'', ''Abstract'', ''Conspectus'', ''Synopsis'', ''Summary'', ''Syllabus'', ''Brief'', ''Digest''. An ''abridgment'' is a work shortened by condensation of}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
'''To set abroach.''' {{smaller|(''a'') To set running; cause to flow or let out liquor, as a cask or barrel.}}
{{smaller block|Barrels of ale ''set abroach'' in different places of the road had kept the populace in perfect love and loyalty towards the Queen and her favourite. {{right|''Scott'', Kenilworth, II. xi.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Figuratively, to give rise to; spread abroad; disseminate; propagate.}}
{{smaller block|What mischiefs he might ''set abroach.''
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iv. 2.}}}}

'''abroach†''' (a̤-brōch′), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''abrochen'', broach, tap, < OF. ''brocher, brochier'', broach, with prefix ''a-'', due to adv. ''abroche'': see ''abroach, prep. phr.'', and ''broach.''] To open, as a cask, for the purpose of letting out liquor; tap; broach.
{{smaller block|Thilke tonne that I schal ''abroche.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Wife of Bath, Prol., l. 177.}}}}

'''abroad''' (a̤-brâd′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' or ''a.'' [< ME. ''abroad, abrod,'' < ''a''<sup>3</sup>, ''prep.'', on, + ''brood, brod,'' broad: see ''broad.''] '''1.''' Broadly; widely; expansively; outward on all or on both sides.
{{smaller block|The love of God is shed ''abroad'' in our hearts. {{right|Rom. v. 5.}}
Her winges bothe ''abrod'' she spradde. {{right|''Gower.''}}
<poem>Look now ''abroad''—another race has filled
These populous borders.</poem>{{right|''Bryant'', The Ages, st. 32.}}}}
'''2.''' Out of or beyond certain limits. {{smaller|(''a'') Beyond the walls of a house or the bounds of any inclosure: as, to walk ''abroad.''}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Where as he lay
So sick alway,
He myght not come ''abrode.''</poem>
{{right|''Sir T. More'', A Merry Jest.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|We are for the most part more lonely when we go ''abroad'' among men than when we stay in our chambers.
{{right|''Thoreau'', Walden, p. 147.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Beyond the bounds of one’s own country; in foreign countries: as, he lived ''abroad'' for many years. [In the United States used most commonly with reference to Europe.]}}
{{smaller block|At home the soldier learned how to value his rights, ''abroad'' how to defend them.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hallam’s Const. Hist.}}}}
{{smaller block|Others, still, are introduced from ''abroad'' by fashion, or are borrowed thence for their usefulness.
{{right|''F. Hall'', Mod. Eng., p. 153.}}}}
'''3.''' Absent; gone away, especially to a considerable distance: as, the head of the firm is ''abroad.''—'''4.''' In an active state; astir; in circulation: as, there are thieves ''abroad;'' rumors of disaster are ''abroad.''
{{smaller block|There’s villainy ''abroad:'' this letter will tell you more.
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To be all abroad.''' (''a'') To be wide of the mark, in a figurative sense; be far wrong in one’s guess or estimate. (''b'') To be at a loss; be puzzled, perplexed, bewildered, nonplussed; be all or quite at sea.—'''The schoolmaster is abroad,''' education is diffused among the people: often used ironically or punningly, implying that the schoolmaster is absent. See ''schoolmaster.''}}

'''Abrocoma''' (ab-rok′ọ̄-mä̤), ''n.'' Same as ''Habrocoma.''

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''abrocome''' (ab′rọ̄-kōm), ''n.'' Same as ''habrocome.''

'''abrogable''' (ab′rọ̄-ga̤-bl), ''a.'' [< L. as if *''abrogabilis,'' < ''abrogare,'' abrogate: see ''abrogate, v.,'' and ''-ble.''] Capable of being abrogated.

'''abrogate''' (ab′rọ̄-gāt), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''abrogated,'' ppr. ''abrogating.'' [< L. ''abrogatus,'' pp. of ''abrogare,'' annul, repeal, < ''ab,'' from, + ''rogare,'' ask, propose a law: see ''rogation.''] '''1.''' To abolish summarily; annul by an authoritative act; repeal. {{smaller|Applied specifically to the repeal of laws, customs, etc., whether expressly or by establishing something inconsistent therewith. See ''abrogation.''}}
{{smaller block|The supremacy of mind ''abrogated'' ceremonies.
{{right|''Bancroft,'' Hist. U. S., II. 346.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Since I revoke, annul, and ''abrogate''
All his decrees in all kinds: they are void!</poem>
{{right|''Browning,'' Ring and Book, II. 170.}}}}
'''2†.''' To keep clear of; avoid.
{{smaller block|''Perge,'' good master Holofernes, ''perge;'' so it shall please you to ''abrogate'' scurrility. {{right|''Shak.,'' L. L. L., iv. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|= '''Syn. 1.''' ''Abolish, Repeal, Rescind,'' etc. (see ''abolish''), cancel, invalidate, dissolve, countermand.}}

'''abrogate†''' (ab′rọ̄-gāt), ''a.'' [< L. ''abrogatus,'' annulled, pp. of ''abrogare:'' see ''abrogate, v.''] Annulled; abolished.

'''abrogation''' (ab-rọ̄-gā′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''abrogatio(n-),'' a repeal, < ''abrogare,'' repeal: see ''abrogate, v.''] The act of abrogating. {{smaller|Specifically, the annulling of a law by legislative action or by usage. See ''derogation.'' Abrogation is ''expressed'' when pronounced by the new law in general or particular terms; it is ''implied'' when the new law contains provisions positively contrary to the former law.}}
{{smaller block|There are no such institutions here;—no law that can abide one moment when popular opinion demands its ''abrogation.'' {{right|''W. Phillips,'' Speeches, p. 47.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
'''abruptness''' (ạ-brupt′nes), ''n.'' The state or quality of being abrupt. {{smaller|(''a'') The state or quality of being broken off, steep, or craggy; sudden breach of continuity; precipitousness. (''b'') Suddenness; unceremonious haste or vehemence. (''c'') Any want of continuity or smoothness.}}
{{smaller block|Some other languages, for their soft and melting fluency, as having no ''abruptness'' of consonants, have some advantage of the English.
{{right|''Howell'', Forreine Travell, p. 158.}}}}

'''Abrus''' (ā′brus), ''n.'' [NL., prop. *''Habrus'', < Gr. ἁβρός, graceful, elegant, delicate.] A small genus of leguminous plants. {{smaller|''A. precatorius'', or Indian licorice, is a woody twiner, indigenous to India, but now found in all tropical countries, where its root is often used as a substitute for licorice. Its polished, party-colored seeds, of the size of a small pea, called ''crabs'-eyes'', ''jumble-beads'', and ''jequirity'' or ''John Crow beans'', are employed for rosaries, necklaces, etc., and as a remedy in diseases of the conjunctiva. They have given their native name of ''retti'' [Hind. ''ratti'', ''ratī''] to a weight (2.1875 grains) used by Hindu jewelers and druggists. See ''retti-weights''.}}

'''abs-'''. A prefix of Latin origin; a form of ''ab-'', used (as in Latin) before ''c'', ''q'', ''t'', as in ''abscond'', ''abstain'', ''absterge'', ''abstract'', etc.

'''abscess''' (ab′ses), ''n.'' [< L. ''abscessus'', a going away, in medical language an abscess, < ''abscedere'', go away, < ''abs'', lengthened form of ''ab'', away, + ''cedere'', go: see ''cede''.] In ''med.'', a collection of pus in the tissues of any part of the body.

'''abscessed''' (ab′sest), ''p. a.'' Diseased with an abscess or with abscesses.

'''abscession†''' (ab-sesh′o̤n), ''n.'' [< L. ''abscessio''(''n''-), < ''abscedere'', go away; see ''abscess''.] '''1.''' Departure.
